        ":question/problem": "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}$ at the point where $x=3$ is{{field-1}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2{x}^{2}+6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x+6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (4x+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2x+3)}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+3}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(\\frac{2x+3}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(3)+6}{2\\sqrt{2(3{)}^{2}+6(3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
