        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-5{x}^{2}+9x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9-10x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (9-10x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-10(1)}{2\\sqrt{-5(1{)}^{2}+9(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
